Scott asks whether there is a non-trivial combinator of type \(\mathbb{P}(D\to E)\to(\mathbb{P}D\to\mathbb{P}E)\), whether \((D\to\mathbb{P}E)\), \(\mathbb{P}(D\times E)\), and \(\mathbb{P}D\times\mathbb{P}E\) are in general isomorphic, whether there is a non-trivial combinator of type \(\mathbb{P}(D\times E)\times\mathbb{P}(E\times F)\to\mathbb{P}(D\times F)\), and whether \(\mathbb{P}\mathbf{N}\) is connected with \(P\mathbf{N}\). `papplyEval` is the approximable evaluation lift relating power-domain neighbourhoods \(\Phi\), \(A\), and \(B\) when every function neighbourhood in \(\Phi\) and every argument neighbourhood in \(A\) admit an evaluation witness in \(B\); the three masters are related because neighbourhoods drawn from them evaluate into the master of the codomain, an intersection of result neighbourhoods is witnessed by intersecting the two evaluation witnesses, and smaller inputs with a larger output keep that witness. `papplyEval_rel` is this condition together with the three power-domain memberships. `papplyB` packages the lift as a map out of the product \(\mathbb{P}(D\to E)\times\mathbb{P}D\), and `papply` is its curry, which has the type of the first question. `papplyEval_step_witness` shows the lift is non-degenerate: the up-set of the step neighbourhood of maps sending \(X_0\) into \(Y_0\), the up-set of \(X_0\), and the up-set of \(Y_0\) are related, with \(Y_0\) the witness because every map in that step sends \(X_0\) into \(Y_0\) and is monotone. `papplyEval_rel_Ypd_iff` rewrites the relation on codes as a bounded \(\forall\forall\exists\) of evaluation on the three decoded lists, and `re_forallG_forallX_existsY` shows those quantifiers preserve recursive enumerability by primitive-recursive reindexing around the bounded existential and universal lemmas, which is `papplyEval_isComputable`. `example_721` is the conjunction of that enumerability theorem, the code biconditional, and the step instance. The isomorphism question, the relational-composition combinator, and the comparison of \(\mathbb{P}\mathbf{N}\) with \(P\mathbf{N}\) are not treated. **Theorem:** partial — only the combinator \(\mathbb{P}(D\to E)\to(\mathbb{P}D\to\mathbb{P}E)\) is constructed, and the step instance shows it does not send every triple to the empty relation. **Proof:** partial — approximability of the lift, the code quantifiers, and preservation of enumerability are proved, and the other three questions have no argument.
